1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,860
Monday morning.

2
00:00:01,220 --> 00:00:04,020
You're brushing your teeth, half asleep, staring into

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:04,360
the mirror.

4
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,640
And then the mirror is empty.

5
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:09,580
The toothbrush is still there, floating, moving up

6
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:09,900
and down.

7
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:11,400
Your T-shirt is there.

8
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,080
But you're not.

9
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:14,580
You turned invisible.

10
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:17,440
And then, a second later, everything goes black.

11
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:18,400
You can't see.

12
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:19,400
Not the mirror.

13
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:20,240
Not the sink.

14
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:20,960
Nothing.

15
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,000
Here's the catch nobody puts in the movies.

16
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:26,740
To see, your eyes have to catch light.

17
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:28,660
Light comes in, hits the back of your

18
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:29,720
eye, and stops there.

19
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:32,180
That's what tells your brain something's out there.

20
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:35,060
But if you're invisible, light goes straight through

21
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:35,520
you.

22
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:36,700
Including your eyes.

23
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:37,820
Nothing stops.

24
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:38,900
Nothing gets caught.

25
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:42,000
So a truly invisible person would be completely

26
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,380
blind.

27
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,120
So what if you could turn invisible?

28
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:46,920
Let's find out what would really happen.

29
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,000
So for this story, your power has one

30
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:50,540
exception.

31
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:52,440
Your eyes stay visible.

32
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:53,760
You can see again.

33
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:54,860
You look in the mirror.

34
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:58,420
Two eyeballs float above an empty T-shirt, staring

35
00:00:58,420 --> 00:00:59,220
back at you.

36
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:00,620
Your mum opens the door.

37
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:01,340
She screams.

38
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:02,140
You scream.

39
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:04,000
The toothbrush falls in the sink.

40
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:05,960
That's going to be a problem.

41
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,340
So why aren't we see-through already?

42
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:10,660
A glass of water is.

43
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:12,460
Water is the same all the way through,

44
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,200
so light passes straight across it.

45
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:15,900
But your body is a mix.

46
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,820
Water, fat and proteins all bend light by

47
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:19,900
slightly different amounts.

48
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:21,940
Every time light crosses from one to the

49
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:23,040
next, it bends a bit.

50
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:25,900
After millions of tiny bends, it's scattered everywhere.

51
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,020
And you look solid.

52
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:29,860
Some animals have figured this out.

53
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,300
Meet the glass frog.

54
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:34,200
Its skin and muscles are nearly see-through.

55
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:35,340
The problem is blood.

56
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:37,540
Red blood is very easy to see.

57
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:39,900
So when it sleeps, the glass frog hides

58
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,020
almost ninety percent of its red blood cells

59
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:42,920
inside its liver.

60
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,280
That makes it two to three times more

61
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:45,840
transparent.

62
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,200
Scientists still aren't sure how it does this

63
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,320
without its blood clotting.

64
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:53,220
And in 2024, scientists at Stanford did something

65
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:55,140
wild with food colouring.

66
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:56,720
They rubbed a yellow food dye on the

67
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,020
skin of a living mouse.

68
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,500
And the skin turned see-through.

69
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:01,960
They could watch its insides working.

70
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,780
When they washed the dye off, the skin

71
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:04,480
went back to normal.

72
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:06,920
Before you raid the kitchen, it hasn't been

73
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:07,740
tested on people.

74
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:10,060
And human skin is much thicker.

75
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:15,240
In 2006, engineers built the first real invisibility

76
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:15,500
cloak.

77
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:17,920
It was smaller than your hand, made of

78
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,720
rings of special material that bent waves around

79
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:23,160
a copper cylinder, like water around a rock.

80
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:24,180
It worked.

81
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,220
But only for microwaves.

82
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,380
To your eyes, it was a very obvious

83
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:29,740
pile of circuit boards.

84
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:33,060
So you learn to wear sunglasses.

85
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:34,380
And you learn the rules.

86
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:37,020
Invisible isn't the same as undetectable.

87
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:38,340
You still give off heat.

88
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:39,980
About as much as an old light bulb.

89
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,260
A thermal camera would see you glowing like

90
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:43,740
a lamp, even in total darkness.

91
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:45,920
You still smell like you.

92
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,640
A dog can have up to three hundred

93
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:49,620
million smell sensors in its nose.

94
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:51,360
You have about six million.

95
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,180
And if it rains, every drop that hits

96
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,220
you draws your outline in the air.

97
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:59,880
Then, on Friday night, you see them again.

98
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:01,920
The stranger from the frozen street.

99
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:03,260
The one who waved.

100
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,900
You turn invisible, put on your sunglasses, and

101
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:07,440
follow them through the night market.

102
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:09,160
They stop at the noodle stall.

103
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,160
They turn into a dark alley.

104
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:12,300
You follow.

105
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:13,740
Their dog stops.

106
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:14,560
Sniffs.

107
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:16,300
Looks straight at you.

108
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:18,720
The stranger takes out something like a camera

109
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,120
and points it your way.

110
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:22,500
On the screen, you're glowing.

111
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:23,620
Bright orange.

112
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,380
Like a lamp.

113
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:25,520
They smile.

114
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:26,960
Nice sunglasses.

115
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,260
We've been watching you since the lake.

116
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,800
So if you could turn invisible, where's the

117
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:33,540
first place you'd go?

118
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:34,680
Tell us in the comments.

119
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,480
And subscribe, because next time, you find out

120
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:38,980
who they are.
